## INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have received great attention owing to their many useful properties and applications such as gas storage, separation, catalysis, and sensing.l-ll Generally, their useful properties primarily originate from their components and structures. Therefore, modifying the specificities of the incorporated building blocks and modulating the structural features are vital for fine-tuning their properties, and thus, applications. Within this context, the conjugation of more than two MOFs having dis. similar components and/or structures through the MOF-on-MOF growth process is an attractive strategy for producing hybrid $\mathbf{MOFs^{12-23}}$ having complex, well-designed, or unique properties, which result from their heterogeneous components and structures. Several hybrid MOFs have displayed excellent properties, and the limitations of individual MOFs can be compensated by forming hybrid MOFs.14,15,22-24
Some hybrid MOFs were produced by the isotropic growth of a secondary MOF that have dissimilar components but well-matched cell latices (structure） with a template MOF.12-17 Typically, the growth of a secondary MOF with well-matched cell lattices results in the formation of normal core-shell-type hybrid through iso tropic MOF growth in all directions (or surfaces) of the template MOF.12-17 However, the growth of a secondary MOF having mismatched cellattices typically results in abnormally shaped particles such as a sandwich-type and a tubular-type, through anisotropic MOF growth only in a specific direction (or surface) of the tem plate MOF.16-22 Particularly, producing well-defined hybrid MOFs from two MOFs having mismatched cell lattices through anisotropic MOF-on-MOF growth is quite challenging. Recently, Eddaoudi and co-workers reported the epitaxial growth for the MOFon-MOF heterostructured thin films.18 Woll and co-workers reported the construction of heterogeneous MOF films with huge lattice mismatches.9 Moreover, our group has demonstrated some hybrid MOFs formed via anisotropic MOF growth with mismatched cell lattices. For example, the anisotropic growth of InMIL-88B (MIL: Materials of Institut Lavoisier) on the surface of Fe-MIL-88B hexagonal rods only in the c-direction, where In-MIL88B and Fe-MIL-88B have similar structural topologies but slightly mismatched cell lattices, resulted in the formation of a sandwichtype hybrid MOF (In-MIL-88B/Fe-MIL-88B/In-MIL-88B).% In addition, the anisotropic growth of MOF-NDC on the surface of the six ac planes of an In-MIL-68 hexagonal rod template, where MOF-NDC and In-MIL-68 have completely different structural topologies, resulted in the production of semi-tubular-type In-MIL68@MOF-NDC hybrid.7 However, additional insights on the MOF-on-MOF growth behaviour between two MOFs having mismatched cell lattices are necessary for the fine-controlled for mation of well-organized hybrid MOFs especially with heterogeneous structural features. Herein, we report the construction of coreshell-type hybrid MOFs through the isotropic or anisotropic growth of MIL-88B or MIL-88C structure with dissimilar components and/or cell lattices on the surface of a MIL-88B template. The growth of Ga-MIL-88B on a Fe-MIL-88B template (having well-matched cell lattices but dissimilar components) occurs isotropically, producing a core-shell-type hybrid MOF. In addition, the growth of Fe-MIL-88C on a MIL-88B template (with
mismatched cell lattices and dissimilar components) also results in unexpectedly well-defined core-shell-type hybrids of singly shelled Fe-MIL-88B $@$ Fe-MIL-88C and doubly shelled Fe-MIL-88B@Ga MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C, which appear to be formed via an isotropic manner but is actually formed via an anisotropic manner. The monitoring of structural and compositional changes during the MOF-on-MOF growth process revealed a unique tip-to-middle anisotropic growth process accompanying unprecedented selfadjustment and self-reversion of the MOF cell lattices, which eventually produced well-defined core-shell-type hybrid MOFs via an anisotropic growth.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
First, nanosized hexagonal rods of Fe-MIL-88B (Figure 1A) were prepared via a reported solvothermal reaction25 of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{{H_{2}B D C}}\right)$ to use them as templates for the additional growth of a secondary MOF. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of the resulting hexagonal nanorods revealed the formation of MIL-88B structure (Figures 1D and S1A). MIL-88B has a three-dimensional (3D) hexagonal structure, in which tri-metal units of $\mathbf{M}_{3}\mathbf{O}$ are linked by $\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}$ to form a final 3D structure of $\left[\mathrm{M}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})\right]_{n}26$ (Scheme 1). A similarsolvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{2}N D C\rangle}$ ） was conducted to achieve relatively large microsized Fe-MIL-88C hexagonal rods (Figure 1B). The PXRD pattern of the product confirmed the formation of MIL-88C structure (Figures 1D and SiB), which is technically equivalent to the MIL-88B structure but has slightly long organic linkers (Scheme 1), and thus, different cell parameters $\left(a=10.22\AA\right.$ $c=23.60\mathrm{\AA}$ ， $V=2020\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})^{26}$ compared with those of MIL-88B ( $\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\cdot}{a}}=\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\cdot}{\longrightarrow}}$ $11.05\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ， $c=18.99\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ， $V=1980\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\mathrm{~}$ ).26 The cell parameter values show that MIL-88B and MIL-88C have large mismatched cell lattices, especially in the c direction.
To investigate the growth behaviour of a secondary MOF with large mismatched cell lattices, the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{H_{2}N D C}$ in the presence of Fe-MIL-88B templates was conducted. Either no effective MOF growth or an anisotropic MOF growth was firstly expected due to their large mismatched cell lattices. However, the above reaction resulted in weldefined elongated hexagonal rods (Figure lC), seemingly generated via isotropic growth. First, the particle size of the product was undoubtedly larger than that of Fe-MIL-88B templates (no bare FeMIL-88B template remained) but much smaller than that of the pure Fe-MIL-88C particles generated in the absence of the templates. From this observation, the effective and selective MOF-onMOF growth was recognized without the extra formation of pure
Fe-MIL-88C. Second, the particle length of product was signifi cantly larger and the thickness was slightly higher than those of the original template; this indicated the formation of a well-defined core-shell structure, which is usually generated by isotropic growth The particle size distributions measured from the scanning electron microscopy(SEM)images were $0.88\pm0.10$ ， $1.79\pm0.13$ ,and 5.41 $\pm0.40~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ for Fe-MIL-88B template, core-shell Fe-MIL-88B@FeMIL-88C, and pure Fe-MIL-88C, respectively (Figure S2). The formation of a well-defined core-shell structure is quite unusual in the case of the MOF-on-MOF growth with mismatched cell lattices between core and shell materials. To monitor the formation pro cess of an unexpected core-shellstructure, a series of SEM images of the samples taken at different time points during the reaction were achieved. The SEM images in Figure 2 visually display the MOF-on-MOF (Fe-MIL-88C on Fe-MIL-88B) growth proces during the formation of a core-shell hybrid of Fe-MIL-88B@FeMIL-88C. A gradual particle size increase indicated the steady growth of a secondary MOF on the template. In detail, the growth of Fe-MIL-88C mostly started at both the tips of the template and then effectively propagated in the c direction (toward the centre and outside of the template; Figure 2J), resulting in the gradual covering of the template from the tip to the middle and an overall size increase. The SEM images of the samples taken at 8O and $90~\mathrm{s}$ show the intermediates (highlighted in green for newly grown shell portions in Figures 2E and 2F), which exhibit the incompletely covered shell at the middle of the way to the fully covered coreshell Fe-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C. Based on these observations, a tip-to-middle MOF-on-MOF growth (covering) process with mismatched cell lattices (Figure 2J) was suggested for the unprecedented formation of a well-defined core-shell Fe-MIL-88B@FeMIL-88C through anisotropic growth. A schematic representation for the formation mechanism of a core-shell structure via the tipto-middle anisotropic growth was also proposed (Figure S3). As a control experiment, the growth of Fe-MIL-88B on a Fe-MIL-88C template and thus the formation of Fe-MIL-88C@Fe-MIL-88B was tried by conducting the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ in the presence of Fe-MIL-88C templates; however, no core-shell Fe-MIL-88C@Fe-MIL-88B was generated from the reaction. Instead, a mixture of the unreacted Fe-MIL-88C templates and pure Fe-MIL-88B rods were resulted (Figure S4).
The PXRD and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns of the product confirmed that the newly grown MOF shell was MIL-88C. First of all, the PXRD pattern shows two characteris tic sets of PXRD peaks for MIL-88B and MIL-88C, which indicate the coexistence of MIL-88B and MIL-88C structures (Figure 1D). The peaks at $2\theta=9.24\$ ,9.54,and $10.58^{\circ}$ were assigned to the
(002), (100),and (101） planes of the initial MIL-88B core,and those at $2\theta=7.50$ ,11.20,14.94, and $15.38^{\circ}$ were assigned to the (002), (101), (004), and (103) planes of the newly grown MIL88C shell. The cell parameters of the two MOFs within a core-shell hybrid calculated from the PXRD pattern by whole pattern fitting (MDI Jade 9.0 software) were $a=10.78\textrm{\AA}$ and $c=19.13\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for FeMIL-88B core and $a=9.71\textrm{\AA}$ and $c=23.80\mathrm{\AA}$ for Fe-MIL-88C shell. These values are similar to those of the initial Fe-MIL-88B template( $\overset{\prime}{a}=10.86\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}$ and $c=19.19\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ ) and pure MIL-88C ${\bf\zeta}_{a}=10.03$ A and $c=23.82\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ） calculated from their PXRD patterns by whole pattern fitting (Table S1). The $d_{002}$ values of initial Fe-MIL-88B and pure Fe-MIL-88C obtained from their SAED patterns were 0.94 and $1.16~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figures 3A' and $\mathbf{B^{\prime}}$ ), which gives $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ cell parameters of 18.7 and $23.3\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ ，respectively. Further, we obtained two SAED patterns from a single core-shell Fe-MIL${88}\mathrm{B}@\mathrm{Fe-MIL-88C}$ particle. The SAED pattern obtained from the end of a core-shell particle corresponded to Fe-MIL-88C, as evidenced by a $d_{002}$ value of $1.14~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3C').However, two sets of SAED patterns corresponding to the core and shell were simultaneously measured at the middle of a Fe-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C particle, with $d_{002}$ values of 0.94 and $1.14~\mathrm{nm}$ originating from FeMIL-88B core and Fe-MIL-88C shell, respectively (Figure $3{\mathrm{C}}^{\prime\prime}$ ； these values are consistent with those obtained for pure Fe-MIL88B and Fe-MIL-88C, respectively. The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of Fe-MIL-88B,Fe-MIL-88C, and Fe-MIL-88B $@$ Fe-MIL-88C revealed the typical multilayer sorption behavior, as shown in Figure S5. There was no significant change in the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption property of Fe-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C compared to pure Fe-MIL-88B and Fe-MIL-88C. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET） surface area of Fe-MIL-88B, Fe-MIL-88C, and Fe-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C were 144.0, 17.3, and $33.9\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively.
Next, the PXRD patterns of a series of the samples taken at different time points during the reaction were measured to determine the structural change occurring during the growth process. First, a series of PXRD patterns revealed that the intensities of the characteristic peaks of MIL-88C shell gradually increased with increasing reaction time, which indicated an increase in the MIL-88C shell portion (Figure 4). Furthermore, a series of PXRD patterns provided important information related to the fascinating structural change. Particularly, at the beginning of the growth, the peaks cor responding to the (101） and (103) planes of the MIL-88C shell were detected at slightly lower angles compared with those of pure MIL-88C, indicating that the MIL-88C shell within a hybrid has a high a cell parameter compared with that of pure MIL-88C (because the peaks corresponding to the (0o2) and (0o4) planes of the MIL-88C shell were detected at the similar locations with those of pure MIL-88C). While in the middle and last growth stages, these peaks gradually shifted again to higher angles (highlighted in purple and grey in Figure 4). This peak shift indicated the change of the a cell parameter to low value (because the peaks corresponding to the (002) and (004) planes of the MIL-88C shellhighlighted in red and pink did not noticeably shift from $2\theta=7.48$ and $14.92^{\circ}\ .$ ).The cell parameters of a series of the samples taken at different time points during the growth process were calculated by whole pattern fitting (Table S1). Indeed, the a cell parameter of samples decreased from $10.51\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ at the beginning $(60~\mathrm{s})$ of growth to $9.71\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ at the end (60o s) of the growth. At the beginning of growth, the $a$ (and thus, $b$ ) cell parameter of the MIL-88C shell (10.51 A) was larger than that of pure MIL-88C (10.03 A) but similar to that of the MIL-88B core (template, 10.72 A) possibly because the cell parameter self-adjusted according to the template for the effective growth, especially on the ab planes exposed at the particle tips. This is well consistent with the fact that the growth of Fe-MIL-88C started at the both tips of the template. Nonetheless, it was proba bly unnecessary to continuously maintain the adjusted uncomfort able configuration after a successful anchoring on the template, and thus, the cell parameters returned to its original comfortable configuration during the middle of the growth process.
As a control experiment, the solvothermal reaction of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ biphenyldicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{{H_{2}B P D C}}\right)$ and $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ in the presence of Fe-MIL-88B templates was also conducted to obtain FeMIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88D through the growth of Fe-MIL-88D on a
Fe-MIL-88B template; however, the reaction resulted in a mixture of the unreacted Fe-MIL-88B templates and pure Fe-MIL-88D rods instead of Fe-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88D (Figure S6). The lattice mismatches of Fe-MIL-88C and Fe-MIL-88D compared to FeMIL-88B were 7.6 and $14.0\%$ ，respectively (these lattice mismatch values were calculated based on the a cell parameter values obtained from their PXRD patterns by whole pattern fitting). The lattice mismatches based on the c cell parameter of Fe-MIL-88C and Fe-MIL-88D compared to Fe-MIL-88B were 24.1 and $45.2\%$ ， respectively. A large lattice mismatch between Fe-MIL-88D and Fe-MIL-88B should not allow the effective epitaxial growth of FeMIL-88D on a Fe-MIL-88B template.
To visually confirm the tip-to-middle anisotropic growth of MIL-88C on the MIL-88B template by elemental line-scanning and elemental mapping, the growth of Fe-incorporated MIL-88C on the surface of Ga-incorporated MIL-88B was planned to take ad vantage of the composition difference between the core (Ga) and shell (Fe). However, Ga-MIL-88B cannot be directly generated from the normal solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{H_{2}B D C}$ . The solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ resulted in a completely different MIL $68^{27,28}$ instead of MIL-88B {MIL-68 has a 3D hexagonal structure consisting of a one-dimensional (1D） met al ion chain in the c direction and a 1D metal ion chain connected by $\mathrm{BDC}^{2\cdot}$ to form a 3D structure of $[\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{BDC})]_{n}\}$ .And therefore, core-shell-type hybrid Fe-MIL-88B@Ga-MIL-88B par ticles were firstly prepared by the isotropic growth of Ga-MIL-88B on Fe-MIL-88B template, as reported before.16 The isotropic growth of Ga-MIL-88B on Fe-MIL-88B occurred because of their well-matched cell lattices.16 The particle size slightly increased after the growth of Ga-MIL-88B, which indicated the formation of FeMIL-88B@Ga-MIL-88B (Figure SB). The PXRD pattern of FeMIL-88B@Ga-MIL-88B was identical to that of MIL-88B (Figure 5B'). Moreover, compositional analysis confirmed the additional growth of Ga-incorporated MOF. The energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectrum revealed the incorporation of Ga element within the product (Figure S8B), and the elemental line-scanning profiles confirmed the formation of a core-shell-type hybrid of Fe-MIL
88B@Ga-MIL-88B (Figure $\mathrm{5B^{\prime\prime}}$ ). Although Ga element was present in the entire particle, it was dominant at the outside of particle, which is typical for the shell component of a core-shell structure. In contrast, Fe element was only present at the centre of the particle as the component of a core and was not detected at the outside of particle (Figure $\left\langle5\mathbf{B}^{\prime\prime}\right\rangle$ ). Finally, the elemental mapping images clearly confirmed the formation of a well-defined core-shell-type hybrid Fe-MIL-88B@Ga-MIL-88B (Figure S9).
In the last step, Fe-MIL-88C was grown on the surface of GaMIL-88B by the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{H_{2}N D C}$ in the presence of Fe-MIL-88B $@$ Ga-MIL-88B template. First, a SEM image shows a significant size increase after the additional growth of Fe-MIL-88C on the template and the formation of a well-defined core-shell structure with a completely covered shell (Figure 5C). Second, structural analysis revealed the presence of MIL-88C in the final product. The PXRD pattern of the product displays two characteristic sets of PXRD peaks corresponding to the initial MIL-88B template and the newly grown MIL-88C shell (Figure $5{\mathrm{C}}^{\prime}$ ).The peaks at $2\theta=9.22$ ，9.52 and $10.56^{\circ}$ were assigned to the (002), (100),and (101) planes of the MIL-88B template, and those at $2\theta$ $=7.44$ ,11.02,14.92, and $15.26^{\circ}$ were assigned to the (0o2), (101), (004),and (103) planes of the newly grown MIL-88C shell (Figure 5C'). The cell parameters of the MIL-88B template and the newly grown MIL-88C shell calculated from the PXRD pattern by whole pattern fitting were $a=10.72\textrm{\AA}$ and $c=19.35\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for MIL-88B core and $a=9.81$ Aand $c=24.24\mathring\mathrm{A}$ for MIL-88C shell.
Finally, compositional analysis of the product revealed the formation of a well-defined doubly shelled core-shell of Fe-MIL88B@Ga-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C, which contained heterogeneous components (metals and organic linkers) and cell lattices in a single particle.First, the EDX spectrum of the product revealed the co-existence of Ga- and Fe-incorporated MOFs (Figure S8C). Second, the elemental line scanning profiles measured in the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ direction confirmed the formation of a well-defined Fe-MIL
88B@Ga-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C.Fe element was dominant both at the centre of the particle as the component of a first MOF (FeMIL-88B) and at the end of the particle as the component of a third MOF (Fe-MIL-88C),whereas Ga element was dominant at the middle region between the centre and end of the particle as the component of a second MOF (Ga-MIL-88B) (Figure $5\mathrm{C^{\prime\prime}}$ ). In addition, the elemental line scanning profiles measured perpendicular to the c direction at the centre of the particle confirmed that Fe-MIL-88C completely covered the entire template (Figure $S10\mathrm{F^{\prime}}$ ). Fe element was dominant at the centre of the particle as the component of a first Fe-MIL-88B and was also present at the out most surface of the particle as the component of a third Fe-MIL88C.Furthermore, Ga element was dominant at the middle region between the centre and end of the particle as the component of a second Ga-MIL-88B (Figure $S10\mathrm{F^{\prime}}$ ). Decisively, the elemental mapping images of the product clearly evidenced the formation of the beautifully arranged Fe-MIL-88B@Ga-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL88C core-shell hybrid (Figure 6E).
The SEM images of a series of the samples taken at different time points during the formation of the doubly shelled Fe-MIL88B@Ga-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C hybrid displayed the tip-to middle MOF-on-MOF growth process of a third MOF (Fe-MIL 88C) (Figure Sll). The particle size of the samples noticeably increased in the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ direction with increasing reaction time. Additionally, the incompletely covered intermediates formed before the creation of a perfectly covered core-shell hybrid were detected in the middle of the growth process. Finally, the tip-to-middle growth process was visually confirmed by the elemental mapping images (Figure 6). At the beginning of the reaction (60 s), a third Fe-MIL88C was only detected at the both tips of the particles (Figure 6B). In the middle of the reaction (80 and 300 s), a third Fe-MIL-88C was also detected at the part of side region in addition to the elongated tips of the template (Figures 6C and D). Lastly, a third FeMIL-88C covered the entire template at the end of the reaction, as shown in Figure 6E. The corresponding PXRD patterns of the samples taken at different time points (Figure Sl2) revealed that the intensity of the characteristic peaks of the MIL-88C shell gradually increased with increasing reaction time, indicating an increment in the MIL-88C shell portion. The cell parameters of the MIL-88B core and MIL-88C shell obtained from the PXRD patterns of a series of the samples by whole pattern fitting are summarized in Table S2.
Numerous MOF materials including hybrid MOFs can be used as precursor materials for the preparation of functional carbonbased materials.29-35 As-prepared Fe-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C and Fe-MIL-88B@Ga-MIL-88B@Fe-MIL-88C were pyrolyzed  at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to convert them to carbon-based materials (Figures S17 and S18).And then, the resulting carbon-based materials were tested in oxygen reduction reaction (ORR).As shown in cyclic voltammetry curves (Figure Sl9), the noticeable redox peaks were not observed in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ -saturated KOH electrolyte but the well-defined cathodic peaks were detected in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated KOH electrolyte, indicating their electrocatalytic activities for ORR. However, no noteworthy ORR activities were observed in these carbon-based materials, as shown in their linear sweep voltammetry data collected using a rotating disk electrode at $1600\mathrm{rpm}$ (Figure S19E).
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, singly and doubly shelled core-shell-type hybrid MOFs were synthesized by the isotropic and/or anisotropic growth of secondary and tertiary MOFs. Particularly, monitoring the anisotropic growth process of MIL-88C on the surface of a MIL-88B template having mismatched cell lattices revealed an interesting tipto-middle growth (covering) process, which resulted in the formation of well-defined core-shell-type hybrid MOF particles despite the anisotropic growth. The early anchoring of MIL-88C on the template through the adjustment of its $a$ and $b$ cell parameters according to those of the template was effectively initiated at the both tips of the MIL-88B template, where the ab plane was exposed, and then the growth was successfully propagated in the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ direction (to the middle of the particle). During the middle of the growth process, the cell parameters of MIL-88C spontaneously reverted to its original comfortable configuration. Eventually, the growth of two parts from the both tips converged in the middle of the particle resulting in the formation of a well-defined core-shell hybrid. The monitoring of structural and compositional changes happening during the reaction confirmed the unique tip-to-middle growth process and demonstrated the fascinating self-adjustment of MOF cell lattices during the growth process.
